If she obeys her husband and goes to the polling booth, that is her personal decision. No one should criticize her. — Compare Romans 14: 4.
What of a country where voting is not mandated by law but feelings run high against those who do not go to the voting booth — perhaps they are exposed to physical danger?
Or what if individuals, while not legally obliged to vote, are severely penalized in some way if they do not go to the polling booth? In these and similar situations, a Christian has to make his own decision.
People should recognize, though, that in matters of individual conscience such as this, each Christian has to make his own decision before Jehovah God. — Romans 14: 12. Whatever personal decisions Jehovah’s Witnesses make in the face of different situations, they take care to preserve their Christian neutrality and freeness of speech.
